Human: How do you become an ordained minister?

Assistant:You can become an ordained minister in your country’s tradition through a number of channels, including but not limited to:

A state or national religious organization, such as the United Church of Christ or the United Methodist Church;
An internet religious organization, such as a blog;
A priest-in-catechism from your tradition;
A local religious leader in your tradition, often requiring approval of previous elders in the congregation;
A graduate of a theological school from your tradition;
A certified public school teacher (this usually only has value in the United States and Australia, I believe);
A clergy member of a religious organization (like a bishop or a deacon in the Episcopal Church in the US, or a deacon in the Orthodox church in the US) who then becomes a licensed minister in your state’s religion; or
Another minister who is ordained and then passes the role on.

These are the most common ways of becoming ordained, and many traditions have their own policies on how best to accomplish this. In the United States, for instance, the process to become an ordained minister involves a certain degree of paperwork, and most traditions will require a background check, sometimes even an appraisal of religious beliefs by a panel of elders in your congregation. As you can see, there are some restrictions (and some opportunities) in becoming ordained. But if you can convince enough people in your community to agree to become an ordained minister of your own faith tradition, it can be really empowering to be an official in that tradition, providing religious education and spiritual support to others.
